{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Router includes several optimizations to ensure your components only re-render when necessary. These optimizations include:"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"structural-sharing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"structural sharing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Router uses a technique called \"structural sharing\" to preserve as many references as possible between re-renders, which is particularly useful for state stored in the URL, such as search parameters."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For example, consider a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"details"},{"type":"text","value":" route with two search parameters, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"foo"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bar"},{"type":"text","value":", accessed like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const search = Route.useSearch()","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When only "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bar"},{"type":"text","value":" is changed by navigating from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/details?foo=f1&bar=b1"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/details?foo=f1&bar=b2"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"search.foo"},{"type":"text","value":" will be referentially stable and only "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"search.bar"},{"type":"text","value":" will be replaced."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"fine-grained-selectors","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fine-grained selectors"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can access and subscribe to the router state using various hooks like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useRouterState"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useSearch"},{"type":"text","value":", and others. If you only want a specific component to re-render when a particular subset of the router state such as a subset of the search parameters changes, you can use partial subscriptions with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" property."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// component won't re-render when `bar` changes\nconst foo = Route.useSearch({ select: ({ foo }) => foo })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"structural-sharing-with-fine-grained-selectors","children":[{"type":"text","value":"structural sharing with fine-grained selectors"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" function can perform various calculations on the router state, allowing you to return different types of values, such as objects. For example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const result = Route.useSearch({\n  select: (search) => {\n    return {\n      foo: search.foo,\n      hello: `hello ${search.foo}`,\n    }\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Although this works, it will cause your component to re-render each time, since "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" is now returning a new object each time it’s called."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can avoid this re-rendering issue by using \"structural sharing\" as described above. By default, structural sharing is turned off to maintain backward compatibility, but this may change in v2."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To enable structural sharing for fine grained selectors, you have two options:"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"enable-it-by-default-in-the-router-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enable it by default in the router options:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const router = createRouter({\n  routeTree,\n  defaultStructuralSharing: true,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"enable-it-per-hook-usage-as-shown-here","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enable it per hook usage as shown here:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const result = Route.useSearch({\n  select: (search) => {\n    return {\n      foo: search.foo,\n      hello: `hello ${search.foo}`,\n    }\n  },\n  structuralSharing: true,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"callout","kind":"important","title":"Important","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Structural sharing only works with JSON-compatible data. This means you cannot use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" to return items like class instances if structural sharing is enabled."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In line with TanStack Router's type-safe design, TypeScript will raise an error if you attempt the following:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const result = Route.useSearch({\n  select: (search) => {\n    return {\n      date: new Date(),\n    }\n  },\n  structuralSharing: true,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If structural sharing is enabled by default in the router options, you can prevent this error by setting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structuralSharing: false"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"structural-sharing","text":"structural sharing","level":2},{"id":"fine-grained-selectors","text":"fine-grained selectors","level":2},{"id":"structural-sharing-with-fine-grained-selectors","text":"structural sharing with fine-grained selectors","level":3},{"id":"enable-it-by-default-in-the-router-options","text":"Enable it by default in the router options:","level":4},{"id":"enable-it-per-hook-usage-as-shown-here","text":"Enable it per hook usage as shown here:","level":4}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Router includes several optimizations to ensure your components only re-render when necessary. These optimizations include:\n\n## structural sharing\n\nTanStack Router uses a technique called \"structural sharing\" to preserve as many references as possible between re-renders, which is particularly useful for state stored in the URL, such as search parameters.\n\nFor example, consider a `details` route with two search parameters, `foo` and `bar`, accessed like this:\n\n```tsx\nconst search = Route.useSearch()\n```\n\nWhen only `bar` is changed by navigating from `/details?foo=f1&bar=b1` to `/details?foo=f1&bar=b2`, `search.foo` will be referentially stable and only `search.bar` will be replaced.\n\n## fine-grained selectors\n\nYou can access and subscribe to the router state using various hooks like `useRouterState`, `useSearch`, and others. If you only want a specific component to re-render when a particular subset of the router state such as a subset of the search parameters changes, you can use partial subscriptions with the `select` property.\n\n```tsx\n// component won't re-render when `bar` changes\nconst foo = Route.useSearch({ select: ({ foo }) => foo })\n```\n\n### structural sharing with fine-grained selectors\n\nThe `select` function can perform various calculations on the router state, allowing you to return different types of values, such as objects. For example:\n\n```tsx\nconst result = Route.useSearch({\n  select: (search) => {\n    return {\n      foo: search.foo,\n      hello: `hello ${search.foo}`,\n    }\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAlthough this works, it will cause your component to re-render each time, since `select` is now returning a new object each time it’s called.\n\nYou can avoid this re-rendering issue by using \"structural sharing\" as described above. By default, structural sharing is turned off to maintain backward compatibility, but this may change in v2.\n\nTo enable structural sharing for fine grained selectors, you have two options:\n\n#### Enable it by default in the router options:\n\n```tsx\nconst router = createRouter({\n  routeTree,\n  defaultStructuralSharing: true,\n})\n```\n\n#### Enable it per hook usage as shown here:\n\n```tsx\nconst result = Route.useSearch({\n  select: (search) => {\n    return {\n      foo: search.foo,\n      hello: `hello ${search.foo}`,\n    }\n  },\n  structuralSharing: true,\n})\n```\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Structural sharing only works with JSON-compatible data. This means you cannot use `select` to return items like class instances if structural sharing is enabled.\n\nIn line with TanStack Router's type-safe design, TypeScript will raise an error if you attempt the following:\n\n```tsx\nconst result = Route.useSearch({\n  select: (search) => {\n    return {\n      date: new Date(),\n    }\n  },\n  structuralSharing: true,\n})\n```\n\nIf structural sharing is enabled by default in the router options, you can prevent this error by setting `structuralSharing: false`.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Render Optimizations\n---\n\nTanStack Router includes several optimizations to ensure your components only re-render when necessary. These optimizations include:\n\n## structural sharing\n\nTanStack Router uses a technique called \"structural sharing\" to preserve as many references as possible between re-renders, which is particularly useful for state stored in the URL, such as search parameters.\n\nFor example, consider a `details` route with two search parameters, `foo` and `bar`, accessed like this:\n\n```tsx\nconst search = Route.useSearch()\n```\n\nWhen only `bar` is changed by navigating from `/details?foo=f1&bar=b1` to `/details?foo=f1&bar=b2`, `search.foo` will be referentially stable and only `search.bar` will be replaced.\n\n## fine-grained selectors\n\nYou can access and subscribe to the router state using various hooks like `useRouterState`, `useSearch`, and others. If you only want a specific component to re-render when a particular subset of the router state such as a subset of the search parameters changes, you can use partial subscriptions with the `select` property.\n\n```tsx\n// component won't re-render when `bar` changes\nconst foo = Route.useSearch({ select: ({ foo }) => foo })\n```\n\n### structural sharing with fine-grained selectors\n\nThe `select` function can perform various calculations on the router state, allowing you to return different types of values, such as objects. For example:\n\n```tsx\nconst result = Route.useSearch({\n  select: (search) => {\n    return {\n      foo: search.foo,\n      hello: `hello ${search.foo}`,\n    }\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAlthough this works, it will cause your component to re-render each time, since `select` is now returning a new object each time it’s called.\n\nYou can avoid this re-rendering issue by using \"structural sharing\" as described above. By default, structural sharing is turned off to maintain backward compatibility, but this may change in v2.\n\nTo enable structural sharing for fine grained selectors, you have two options:\n\n#### Enable it by default in the router options:\n\n```tsx\nconst router = createRouter({\n  routeTree,\n  defaultStructuralSharing: true,\n})\n```\n\n#### Enable it per hook usage as shown here:\n\n```tsx\nconst result = Route.useSearch({\n  select: (search) => {\n    return {\n      foo: search.foo,\n      hello: `hello ${search.foo}`,\n    }\n  },\n  structuralSharing: true,\n})\n```\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Structural sharing only works with JSON-compatible data. This means you cannot use `select` to return items like class instances if structural sharing is enabled.\n\nIn line with TanStack Router's type-safe design, TypeScript will raise an error if you attempt the following:\n\n```tsx\nconst result = Route.useSearch({\n  select: (search) => {\n    return {\n      date: new Date(),\n    }\n  },\n  structuralSharing: true,\n})\n```\n\nIf structural sharing is enabled by default in the router options, you can prevent this error by setting `structuralSharing: false`.\n"}
